//? Title: Baillie–PSW: Two Primality Tests With No Common Liar
//? Subtitle: Why primes pass the strong Fermat and strong Lucas tests, a census of the composites that fool each test below ten million, and what the combination costs, with datamunge's algorithms::baillie_psw and its Lucas-sequence building blocks
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A probable-prime test checks an identity that every prime satisfies. Composites that satisfy it
anyway are its *pseudoprimes*, or liars. The Baillie–PSW test combines a strong Fermat test to
base 2, which checks \(2^{n-1}\equiv1\) through its chain of square roots, and a strong Lucas test,
which checks the analogous identity \(U_{n+1}\equiv0\) for a Lucas sequence chosen by Selfridge's
rule. We derive both identities: the first from the fact that \(\pm1\) are the only square roots of
1 modulo a prime, the second from the Frobenius map in \(\mathbb F_{p^2}\). A census of all odd
composites below \(10^7\) finds 162 strong pseudoprimes to base 2 and 178 strong Lucas
pseudoprimes, and no number in both lists. The two kinds of liar have different shapes: a base-2
liar needs \(n-1\) to be divisible by the order of 2 modulo each prime factor \(p\), a Lucas liar
needs \(n+1\) to be divisible by the matching rank in \(\mathbb F_{p^2}\), and the factors of the
two kinds of liar share visibly different amounts of \(p-1\) with \(n-1\). No composite below
\(2^{64}\) passes both, so Baillie–PSW is a deterministic test for 64-bit integers; on 64-bit
primes it costs about 3.8 times a single strong Fermat test, a third of a deterministic
Miller–Rabin test, and on random odd numbers less than either.
)

\toc

> Two identities that every prime satisfies

>> The strong Fermat test

Fermat's little theorem says \(a^{p-1}\equiv1\pmod p\) for a prime \(p\nmid a\). Write
\(n-1=d\cdot2^s\) with \(d\) odd. If \(n\) is prime, the sequence

$$
a^{d},\ a^{2d},\ a^{4d},\ \dots,\ a^{2^sd}=a^{n-1}\equiv1\pmod n
$$
\alttext(a to the d, a to the 2 d, a to the 4 d, and so on up to a to the 2 to the s d, which equals a to the n minus 1, congruent to 1 mod n.)

ends at 1, and each term is the square of the one before. Modulo a prime the only square roots of
1 are \(\pm1\), because \(x^2-1=(x-1)(x+1)\equiv0\) forces one factor to vanish in a field. So the
first 1 in the sequence is either the very first term or is preceded by \(-1\):

$$
a^{d}\equiv1\quad\text{or}\quad a^{2^rd}\equiv-1\ \text{for some }0\le r<s.
$$
\alttext(a to the d is congruent to 1, or a to the 2 to the r d is congruent to minus 1 for some r from 0 to s minus 1.)

An odd composite \(n\) that satisfies this for \(a=2\) is a *strong pseudoprime to base 2*. For a
composite modulus 1 has other square roots (by the Chinese remainder theorem), which is why the
strong test catches many composites that pass \(a^{n-1}\equiv1\) alone.

>> The strong Lucas test

Let \(P,Q\) be integers with discriminant \(D=P^2-4Q\), and let \(\alpha,\beta\) be the roots of
\(x^2-Px+Q\). The Lucas sequences are

$$
U_k=\frac{\alpha^k-\beta^k}{\alpha-\beta},\qquad V_k=\alpha^k+\beta^k,
$$
\alttext(U k equals alpha to the k minus beta to the k, over alpha minus beta. V k equals alpha to the k plus beta to the k.)

integers satisfying \(X_{k+1}=PX_k-QX_{k-1}\) with \(U_0=0,U_1=1,V_0=2,V_1=P\). Now take a prime
\(p\nmid 2QD\) with Jacobi symbol \((D/p)=-1\). Then \(D\) is not a square mod \(p\), the polynomial is
irreducible, and \(\alpha,\beta\) live in the field \(\mathbb F_{p^2}\) as conjugates. The Frobenius
map \(x\mapsto x^p\) is the field's only non-trivial automorphism, so it swaps the roots:
\(\alpha^p=\beta\). Hence \(\alpha^{p+1}=\alpha\beta=Q=\beta^{p+1}\), and

$$
U_{p+1}=\frac{\alpha^{p+1}-\beta^{p+1}}{\alpha-\beta}\equiv0\pmod p .
$$
\alttext(U p plus 1 equals alpha to the p plus 1 minus beta to the p plus 1, over alpha minus beta, which is congruent to 0 mod p.)

This is the Lucas analogue of Fermat's theorem, with \(p+1\) in place of \(p-1\). The strong form
follows the same square-root argument in \(\mathbb F_{p^2}\): writing \(n+1=d\cdot2^s\) with \(d\) odd,
a prime satisfies \(U_d\equiv0\) or \(V_{d2^r}\equiv0\) for some \(0\le r<s\), using
\(U_{2k}=U_kV_k\) and \(V_{2k}=V_k^2-2Q^k\). Selfridge's rule fixes the parameters: \(D\) is the
first of \(5,-7,9,-11,\dots\) with \((D/n)=-1\), \(P=1\), \(Q=(1-D)/4\). (For a perfect square no
such \(D\) exists, so squares are rejected first.) `lucas_sequence_mod()` computes \(U_k\) and \(V_k\)
modulo \(n\) in \(O(\log k)\) doubling and increment steps.

>> The combination

```
BAILLIE-PSW(n):
    trial-divide by the primes up to 37
    if n fails the strong Fermat test to base 2: return composite
    if n is a perfect square: return composite
    choose D, P, Q by Selfridge's rule
    return whether n passes the strong Lucas test with D, P, Q
```

The first block shows the parameters and the two tests on a few numbers: three primes, the
smallest strong pseudoprime to base 2, a Carmichael number, and the two smallest strong Lucas
pseudoprimes.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::print("| \\(n\\) | factors | \\(D\\) | \\(Q\\) | strong Fermat (base 2) | Lucas | strong Lucas | Baillie–PSW |\n"
             "| ---: | --- | ---: | ---: | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n");
  auto yes = [](bool b) { return b ? "pass" : "fail"; };
  for (std::uint64_t n : {97ULL, 5459ULL + 2, 1000000007ULL, 2047ULL, 561ULL, 5459ULL, 5777ULL, 3215031751ULL}) {
    const auto f = alg::factorize(n);
    std::string factors;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < f.size(); ++i) factors += (i ? "·" : "") + std::to_string(f[i]);
    const auto s = alg::selfridge_parameters(n);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", n, factors, s.D, s.Q, yes(alg::is_strong_probable_prime_base(n, 2)),
               yes(alg::lucas_probable_prime(n)), yes(alg::strong_lucas_probable_prime(n)), alg::baillie_psw(n) ? "prime" : "composite");
  }
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| \(n\) | factors | \(D\) | \(Q\) | strong Fermat (base 2) | Lucas | strong Lucas | Baillie–PSW |
| ---: | --- | ---: | ---: | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 97 | 97 | 5 | -1 | pass | pass | pass | prime |
| 5461 | 43·127 | -19 | 5 | fail | fail | fail | composite |
| 1000000007 | 1000000007 | 5 | -1 | pass | pass | pass | prime |
| 2047 | 23·89 | 5 | -1 | pass | fail | fail | composite |
| 561 | 3·11·17 | 13 | -3 | fail | fail | fail | composite |
| 5459 | 53·103 | -7 | 2 | fail | pass | pass | composite |
| 5777 | 53·109 | 5 | -1 | fail | pass | pass | composite |
| 3215031751 | 151·751·28351 | -11 | 3 | pass | fail | fail | composite |
// result_end

Both primes pass every test, as the derivation requires. The composites show the division of
labour. \(2047=23\cdot89\), the smallest strong pseudoprime to base 2, and \(3{,}215{,}031{,}751\),
the smallest number that is a strong pseudoprime to bases 2, 3, 5 and 7 at once, pass the Fermat
side and fail both Lucas tests. \(5459=53\cdot103\) and \(5777=53\cdot109\), the smallest strong
Lucas pseudoprimes, pass the Lucas side and fail the strong base-2 test. The Carmichael number
\(561\) satisfies \(a^{560}\equiv1\) for every base coprime to it, yet fails the strong test,
because \(2^{35}\bmod561\) is neither \(\pm1\) and the chain reaches 1 through a square root of 1
other than \(-1\).

> A census of liars below ten million

The block tests every odd composite \(n<10^7\) with four tests: Fermat to base 2
(\(2^{n-1}\equiv1\)), strong Fermat to base 2, Lucas and strong Lucas (both with Selfridge's
parameters). It counts the liars of each test as \(n\) grows, and the numbers that fool both
strong tests.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/baillie_psw_census.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::uint64_t N = 10000000;
  const auto is_prime = alg::prime_sieve(N);
  std::uint64_t fermat = 0, strong = 0, lucas = 0, slucas = 0, both = 0, carmichael_fermat = 0;
  std::vector<std::uint64_t> first_spsp, first_slpsp;
  std::vector<double> lx, c_fermat, c_strong, c_lucas, c_slucas;
  double next = 1000;
  for (std::uint64_t n = 9; n < N; n += 2) {
    if (!is_prime[n]) {
      const bool f = alg::mod_pow(2, n - 1, n) == 1;
      const bool s = f && alg::is_strong_probable_prime_base(n, 2);
      const bool sl = alg::strong_lucas_probable_prime(n);
      const bool l = sl || alg::lucas_probable_prime(n);
      fermat += f;
      strong += s;
      lucas += l;
      slucas += sl;
      both += s && sl;
      if (s && first_spsp.size() < 8) first_spsp.push_back(n);
      if (sl && first_slpsp.size() < 8) first_slpsp.push_back(n);
    }
    if (static_cast<double>(n) >= next) {
      lx.push_back(std::log10(next));
      for (auto [v, c] : {std::pair{&c_fermat, fermat}, {&c_strong, strong}, {&c_lucas, lucas}, {&c_slucas, slucas}})
        v->push_back(std::log10(1.0 + static_cast<double>(c)));
      next *= 1.25;
    }
  }
  std::print("| odd composites \\(n<10^7\\) that pass | count |\n| --- | ---: |\n");
  std::print("| Fermat, base 2 | {} |\n| strong Fermat, base 2 | {} |\n| Lucas (Selfridge) | {} |\n| strong Lucas (Selfridge) | {} |\n"
             "| strong Fermat base 2 **and** strong Lucas | {} |\n", fermat, strong, lucas, slucas, both);
  auto list = [](const std::vector<std::uint64_t>& v) {
    std::string s;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); ++i) s += (i ? ", " : "") + std::to_string(v[i]);
    return s;
  };
  std::print("\nFirst strong pseudoprimes to base 2: {}.\n\nFirst strong Lucas pseudoprimes: {}.\n", list(first_spsp), list(first_slpsp));

  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .lines(lx, c_fermat, "", dm::RGB{252, 165, 165}, 2.0)
      .lines(lx, c_strong, "", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 2.5)
      .lines(lx, c_lucas, "", dm::RGB{147, 197, 253}, 2.0)
      .lines(lx, c_slucas, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2.5)
      .legend({"Fermat base 2", "strong base 2", "Lucas", "strong Lucas"},
              {dm::RGB{252, 165, 165}, dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, dm::RGB{147, 197, 253}, dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}})
      .x_limits(3, 7)
      .y_limits(0, 3.4)
      .title("Pseudoprimes below N")
      .x_label("log10 N")
      .y_label("log10 (1 + count)")
      .size(900, 500)
      .save_svg("build/baillie_psw_census.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| odd composites \(n<10^7\) that pass | count |
| --- | ---: |
| Fermat, base 2 | 750 |
| strong Fermat, base 2 | 162 |
| Lucas (Selfridge) | 757 |
| strong Lucas (Selfridge) | 178 |
| strong Fermat base 2 **and** strong Lucas | 0 |

First strong pseudoprimes to base 2: 2047, 3277, 4033, 4681, 8321, 15841, 29341, 42799.

First strong Lucas pseudoprimes: 5459, 5777, 10877, 16109, 18971, 22499, 24569, 25199.
\image(figures/baillie_psw_census.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Number of odd composites below \(N\) passing each test, on log scales: Fermat and strong Fermat to base 2 (light and dark red), Lucas and strong Lucas with Selfridge's parameters (light and dark blue).)
\alttext(Line chart of log10 of one plus the count of liars against log10 N from 3 to 7. The Fermat and Lucas curves are close together and reach about 2.9, roughly 750 liars. The strong base-2 and strong Lucas curves start later, at about 2,000 and 5,000, run close together, and reach about 2.2, roughly 170 liars.)

Strengthening each test removes about four fifths of its liars: below \(10^7\) there are 750
Fermat pseudoprimes to base 2 but 162 strong ones, and 757 Lucas pseudoprimes but 178 strong
ones. The first lists agree with the published sequences (OEIS A001262 and A217255). The two
strong families are about equally common and grow at about the same rate, yet not one number
below \(10^7\) is in both. Exhaustive searches have extended this to every \(n<2^{64}\), so for
64-bit integers Baillie–PSW makes no mistakes. Pomerance gave a heuristic argument that
counterexamples exist in infinite number, but none has ever been found.

> Why the liars differ

Let \(n=\prod p_i\) be squarefree. Fermat's congruence \(2^{n-1}\equiv1\pmod n\) holds exactly when,
for each \(p_i\), the multiplicative order of 2 modulo \(p_i\) divides \(n-1\). The Lucas congruence
\(U_{n+1}\equiv0\) holds when, for each \(p_i\), the *rank of apparition* (the least \(k\) with
\(p_i\mid U_k\)) divides \(n+1\). For a prime with \((D/p)=-1\) that rank divides \(p+1\). So a
base-2 liar needs its prime factors' \(p-1\) to share structure with \(n-1\), and a Lucas liar
needs their \(p+1\) to share structure with \(n+1\). A number fooling both would need both at
once, and since \(n-1\) and \(n+1\) share only the factor 2, this is rare enough that no example is
known. The block checks this for the liars found above, measuring each liar's factors \(p\) by
\(\gcd(p-1,n-1)/(p-1)\) and \(\gcd(p+1,n+1)/(p+1)\), the share of \(p\mp1\) that divides \(n\mp1\).

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/baillie_psw_structure.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <numeric>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::uint64_t N = 10000000;
  const auto is_prime = alg::prime_sieve(N);
  std::vector<double> sx, sy, lx, ly;
  for (std::uint64_t n = 9; n < N; n += 2) {
    if (is_prime[n]) continue;
    const bool s = alg::is_strong_probable_prime_base(n, 2);
    const bool l = alg::strong_lucas_probable_prime(n);
    if (!s && !l) continue;
    double minus = 1, plus = 1;  // smallest share over the prime factors
    for (std::uint64_t p : alg::factorize(n)) {
      minus = std::min(minus, static_cast<double>(std::gcd(p - 1, n - 1)) / static_cast<double>(p - 1));
      plus = std::min(plus, static_cast<double>(std::gcd(p + 1, n + 1)) / static_cast<double>(p + 1));
    }
    (s ? sx : lx).push_back(std::log2(minus));
    (s ? sy : ly).push_back(std::log2(plus));
  }
  auto median = [](std::vector<double> v) {
    std::ranges::sort(v);
    return v[v.size() / 2];
  };
  std::print("| liars | count | median \\(\\log_2\\) share of \\(p-1\\) in \\(n-1\\) | median \\(\\log_2\\) share of \\(p+1\\) in \\(n+1\\) |\n"
             "| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::print("| strong base 2 | {} | {:.1f} | {:.1f} |\n", sx.size(), median(sx), median(sy));
  std::print("| strong Lucas | {} | {:.1f} | {:.1f} |\n", lx.size(), median(lx), median(ly));
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .points(sx, sy, "", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 5.0)
      .points(lx, ly, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 5.0)
      .legend({"strong base 2", "strong Lucas"}, {dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}})
      .y_limits(-22, 4)
      .title("Shared structure of the liars' prime factors")
      .x_label("log2 min over p | n of gcd(p-1, n-1)/(p-1)")
      .y_label("log2 min over p | n of gcd(p+1, n+1)/(p+1)")
      .size(900, 600)
      .save_svg("build/baillie_psw_structure.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| liars | count | median \(\log_2\) share of \(p-1\) in \(n-1\) | median \(\log_2\) share of \(p+1\) in \(n+1\) |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| strong base 2 | 162 | -2.8 | -10.4 |
| strong Lucas | 178 | -9.8 | -8.4 |
\image(figures/baillie_psw_structure.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Each liar below \(10^7\) as a point: horizontally, the smallest fraction of \(p-1\) that divides \(n-1\) over its prime factors \(p\); vertically, the same for \(p+1\) and \(n+1\). Both axes are \(\log_2\); 0 means the whole of \(p\mp1\) divides \(n\mp1\).)
\alttext(Scatter plot of the strong liars below 10 million. Red base-2 liars cluster on the right, with most horizontal values between minus 5 and 0 and a few down to minus 12. Blue Lucas liars cluster on the left, from about minus 15 to minus 5. Vertically the red group lies between about minus 16 and minus 5 and the blue group between minus 14 and 0.)

The horizontal axis separates the two families almost completely. For strong base-2 liars
the median share is \(2^{-2.8}\): each prime factor's \(p-1\) mostly divides \(n-1\), and for many
liars all of it does. For strong Lucas liars the median is \(2^{-9.8}\), essentially only the
common factor 2. The vertical axis separates them less cleanly (medians \(2^{-10.4}\) and
\(2^{-8.4}\)). The Lucas condition needs only the rank of apparition, which can be a small divisor
of \(p+1\), to divide \(n+1\), just as the base-2 condition needs only the order of 2 to divide
\(n-1\). Still, the picture shows why a common liar is hard to build: its factors would have to
sit at the right of the red cloud and the top of the blue one at the same time.

> Cost

Each Fermat test takes about \(\log_2 n\) modular squarings. The Lucas test's doubling step updates
\(U\), \(V\) and \(Q^k\), and an increment step adds more multiplications, so it costs a small multiple
of one Fermat test. The block times both, Baillie–PSW, and `is_probable_prime()` (deterministic
Miller–Rabin with a fixed set of bases for 64-bit numbers) on a million random odd 64-bit numbers
and on 100,000 64-bit primes.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <chrono>
#include <random>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::mt19937_64 rng(1);
  std::vector<std::uint64_t> odd(1000000), primes;
  for (auto& v : odd) v = rng() | 1ULL | (1ULL << 63);
  while (primes.size() < 100000) {
    const std::uint64_t v = rng() | 1ULL | (1ULL << 63);
    if (alg::is_probable_prime(v)) primes.push_back(v);
  }
  using clock = std::chrono::steady_clock;
  auto time = [&](const std::vector<std::uint64_t>& xs, auto&& test) {
    std::size_t passed = 0;
    const auto t0 = clock::now();
    for (auto v : xs) passed += test(v);
    const double ns = std::chrono::duration<double, std::nano>(clock::now() - t0).count() / xs.size();
    return std::pair{ns, passed};
  };
  std::print("| test | ns per random odd number | passed | ns per prime | passed |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  auto row = [&](const char* name, auto&& test) {
    const auto [a, pa] = time(odd, test);
    const auto [b, pb] = time(primes, test);
    std::print("| {} | {:.0f} | {} | {:.0f} | {} |\n", name, a, pa, b, pb);
  };
  row("strong Fermat, base 2", [](std::uint64_t v) { return alg::is_strong_probable_prime_base(v, 2); });
  row("strong Lucas", [](std::uint64_t v) { return alg::strong_lucas_probable_prime(v); });
  row("Baillie–PSW", [](std::uint64_t v) { return alg::baillie_psw(v); });
  row("deterministic Miller–Rabin", [](std::uint64_t v) { return alg::is_probable_prime(v); });
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| test | ns per random odd number | passed | ns per prime | passed |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| strong Fermat, base 2 | 408 | 45199 | 437 | 100000 |
| strong Lucas | 1090 | 45199 | 1011 | 100000 |
| Baillie–PSW | 160 | 45199 | 1356 | 100000 |
| deterministic Miller–Rabin | 322 | 45199 | 4813 | 100000 |
// result_end

About 4.5% of random odd 64-bit numbers are prime (the prime number theorem predicts
\(2/\ln2^{63.5}\approx4.5\%\)), and all four tests agreed on every one of the million numbers.
The strong Lucas test costs about 2.7 times a strong Fermat test, so on a prime Baillie–PSW
costs both, about 1.2 µs on this machine, against 3.8 µs for the deterministic Miller–Rabin test,
which runs several bases. On random odd numbers Baillie–PSW is the cheapest test of all, at about
150 ns: trial division by the primes up to 37 removes about 70% of odd numbers at once,
and almost every remaining composite fails the base-2 test, so the Lucas test runs only on
numbers that are very likely prime.

> Summary

- Primes satisfy the strong Fermat condition because \(\pm1\) are the only square roots of 1 in a
  field, and the strong Lucas condition because Frobenius swaps the roots in \(\mathbb F_{p^2}\),
  giving \(U_{p+1}\equiv0\) when \((D/p)=-1\).
- Below \(10^7\) the strong base-2 test has 162 liars and the strong Lucas test 178; none is common
  to both. No common liar exists below \(2^{64}\), so Baillie–PSW is deterministic there.
- The liars' prime factors share most of \(p-1\) with \(n-1\) for the base-2 test and almost none
  for the Lucas test, a structural reason the families do not meet.
- On primes the test costs about 3.8 strong Fermat tests, a third of deterministic Miller–Rabin;
  on random inputs, with trial division first, it is cheaper still.
